  "title": "No trail laid: do the ants find the food themselves?",
  "question": "Nobody laid a trail in either colony. Does the new walk (B) read as ants finding the food on the right and making a road to it on their own?",
  "context_md": "Same colony test, same seed, 140 cells from nest (thin strip, left) to food (red, right). **No hand-laid trail at any point**, which answers your question from last night: every earlier result had one for the first 6,000 frames.\n\n**Magenta** = ant with an empty crop, **cyan** = carrying food. Frames 1 to 6,961, every 240. 20 ants in each, nest-only breeding on so neither colony grows.\n\n- **A, today's walk:** nobody reaches the food; all 20 starve by frame 4,290.\n- **B, the new walk:** first ant reaches the food at frame 2,412; 7 of 20 reach it and carry food back.\n\nOver 24 seeds the new walk makes a median 10.5 / 4.5 / 2 round trips at 90 / 140 / 200 cells with no trail (today's walk 0 at every distance). Most of B's colony still starves; the report's section 13 traces why (the dying sit on the far side of the nest from where food arrives).",
